{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-95077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2xy},x=t$ and $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2+t}{\\sqrt{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y},x=2t$ and $y={t}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}=4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2t)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}=4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =8t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+8{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "4t(1+2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "4t(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "8{t}^{3}(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s"] ["math" "2{sec}^{2}⁡2s"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2s"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s+{csc}^{2}⁡2s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},x={s}^{2},$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 2s+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2s{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s} \\\\  & =2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"] ["math" "2(s+2){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"] ["math" "2s{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"] ["math" "s(s+2){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"] ["math" "{s}^{2}{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1+4{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x})"] ["math" "(1-3{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}})"] ["math" "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})"] ["math" "(\\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{2}})"] ["math" "(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}$ and $y={e}^{-x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}\\cdot (-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1-2{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+1)-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4{x}^{2}(x-6)}{x+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x(3x+4)}{(x+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x(4x-3)}{x+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-95118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"] ["math" "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))"] ["math" "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)"] ["math" "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st).$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st))(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s{cos}^{2}⁡(st)-s{sin}^{2}⁡(st) \\\\  & =s({cos}^{2}⁡(st)-{sin}^{2}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)"] ["math" "s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)"] ["math" "t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)"] ["math" "{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)"] ["math" "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ where $x=t+s$ and $y=ts.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s{e}^{t+s}"] ["math" "s{e}^{t+s}(t+1)"] ["math" "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)"] ["math" "t{e}^{t+s}(s-t)"] ["math" "{e}^{t+s}(s+t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170673" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=2x+xy,$ where $x=s{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "st{e}^{s}"] ["math" "s{e}^{2s}"] ["math" "s{e}^{st}"] ["math" "t{e}^{2s}"] ["math" "t{e}^{st}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s{e}^{2s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2y,$ where $x=s{t}^{2}$ and $y={s}^{3}-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}"] ["math" "4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-5{s}^{2}+2{t}^{4}"] ["math" "7{s}^{3}{t}^{2}-4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6s"] ["math" "7{s}^{5}{t}^{2}+2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+4{t}^{2}"] ["math" "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}$ and $z={t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{{c}_{1}{s}^{2}+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y+z}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{-({s}^{2}+{t}^{2})+2t(s+t)}{(s+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2ts-{s}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=-1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=-1+2+1=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-170675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-37132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2{e}^{y}+4z,$ where $x=t{s}^{2},$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})$ and $z={s}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})}=-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2ts, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}})\\cdot 2ts+(-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}))\\cdot (\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}})+(4)\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-4s+8s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}+4s \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{st+1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}-{s}^{2}}{s}"] ["math" "st({s}^{2}{t}^{2}+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},$ where $x=t+2s,$ $y=3t-s$ and $z=st.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(t+2s)=2t+4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(3t-s)=6t-2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2t+4s)\\cdot 2+(6t-2s)\\cdot (-1)+(2st)\\cdot t \\\\  & =4t+8s-6t+2s+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =10s-2t+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2(5s-t+s{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(5s-t+s{t}^{2})"] ["math" "3s-5t-1"] ["math" "4(st+s-t)"] ["math" "s(t+s)^{2}"] ["math" "t(5-s)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(5s-t+s{t}^{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ where $x=2,$ $y=s{e}^{t}$ and $z=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=xz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2s{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =s{e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(ts{e}^{t+s})\\cdot 0+(2t{e}^{s})\\cdot s{e}^{t}+(2s{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{s} \\\\  & =2ts{e}^{t+s}+2s{e}^{t+s} \\\\  & =2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8194713c58a546b3a89b58adc8494cb9415333032cb9763dd1edd221da5805b"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(t+s){e}^{t+s}"] ["math" "2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}"] ["math" "2t(s+1){e}^{t+s}"] ["math" "4(t-s){e}^{t+s}"] ["math" "4t(s-1){e}^{t+s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "q-94812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b7cdbee86f29dd138d1a36107691dd9d9300c2a040400477b5e075a2827cca"} {"question" "e-8256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8256.json" "file_sha256" "9121b86850a926106b99475eff5b2db164b76e7b44d5dcf4525f34e436607df7"} {"question" "e-8256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8256.json" "file_sha256" "9121b86850a926106b99475eff5b2db164b76e7b44d5dcf4525f34e436607df7"} {"question" "e-8257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8257.json" "file_sha256" "5a2b7a4e357ec33a2baad35f9496881450c88efeef1edbaed357cf20ebbc6097"} {"question" "e-8257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8257.json" "file_sha256" "5a2b7a4e357ec33a2baad35f9496881450c88efeef1edbaed357cf20ebbc6097"} {"question" "e-8258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8258.json" "file_sha256" "6293d5810b9aced11768bcd6b2d246c957a3024b36045f5209b4e06728c845f4"} {"question" "e-8258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8258.json" "file_sha256" "6293d5810b9aced11768bcd6b2d246c957a3024b36045f5209b4e06728c845f4"} {"question" "e-8259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8259.json" "file_sha256" "2e643af06385f8bd467371cec5a863d64289a85b0102dbaa2cc6b662cefaefa1"} {"question" "e-8259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/example-8259.json" "file_sha256" "2e643af06385f8bd467371cec5a863d64289a85b0102dbaa2cc6b662cefaefa1"}] "usage" {"q-37205" [] "e-8256" [] "e-8257" [] "q-95078" [] "e-8258" [] "e-8259" []} "basis" 1655 "retractions" [[17592186167269 :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\ln (xy^{2}), x = \\cos t$ and $y = \\sin t$, compute $\\frac{df}{dt}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{\\pi }{2}$."] [17592186167269 :question/worked-solution "On the given interval, $x,y>0$ and $f=\\ln x+2\\ln y$. Hence $f_x=1/x$ and $f_y=2/y$. The chain rule gives $$\\frac{df}{dt}=\\frac{-\\sin t}{\\cos t}+\\frac{2\\cos t}{\\sin t}=2\\cot t-\\tan t.$$"] [17592186167269 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186167249 :question/problem "Let $f(x, y) = 2x + xy$, where $x = se^{s}$ and $y = te^{s}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$."] [17592186167249 :question/worked-solution "Keep $s$ fixed. Then $x_t=0$, $y_t=e^s$, $f_x=2+y$, and $f_y=x$. Therefore $$f_t=f_xx_t+f_yy_t=(2+y)0+xe^s=(se^s)e^s=se^{2s}.$$"] [17592186167249 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186166517 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t$, and $y=e^t$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}$."] [17592186166517 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/e0bfff900fe36a4bdd1a679c435ccf0b.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t$, where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} =-y\\sin (xy), \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =-x\\sin (xy)\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= -e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}), \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= -t\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1, \\frac{dy}{dt} = e^{t}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dt} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} \\\\\n&= [- e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot 1 + [- t\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&=-e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) - te^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) \\\\\n&= - (1 + t)e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166518 :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}$."] [17592186166518 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166519 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$."] [17592186166519 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6bba4306b4e5823056f9eea8e7d48a2a.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= 2s\\cos t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2t\\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= -s\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= \\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} \\\\\n&= (2s\\cos t)(-s\\sin t) + (2t\\sin s)(\\sin s) \\\\\n&=-2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t + 2t\\sin^{2} s \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - 2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - s^{2}\\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nin the above simplification."] [17592186166520 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z}$, where $x = s + t$, $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$."] [17592186166520 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6edee879a5f94e8ebd640b97917195c1.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where\n\n$$\nx=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)\n$$\n\nand $z = z(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = ye^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = xe^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} = e^{xy + z}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= (s - t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s - t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= (s + t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s + t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} &= e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = e^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$, $y$, and $z$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= 1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= -1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= (s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 1 + (s + t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot (-1) + e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= (s - t - s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186165044 17592186165045 17592186165046 17592186165047 17592186165048 17592186165164 17592186165165 17592186165166 17592186165167 17592186165168] "immutable_fields" [[17592186165043 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165043 :answer-field/id] [17592186165043 :answer-field/key] [17592186165043 :answer-field/type] [17592186165163 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165163 :answer-field/id] [17592186165163 :answer-field/key] [17592186165163 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
